{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CdsoJ4QLZ3qDvAdw5Lgri","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CtYLvibTU7ytDWJWP1SxqA","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-creator-0/87cd2a2d-a4f9-585b-836f-2648df7a6284/scratchpad/e7run/A/e7-p02/primary/exec-ws/analysis/ea-determinacy.md","content":"# E-A determinacy statement: joinspec.md over the probe set (e7-p02)\n\nThe six probes in `package/artifacts/probes.json` exercise exactly four\nbehaviors: separator rendering, item ordering, the empty list, and the\nsingle-item list. For each, this is whether `joinspec.md` determines the\noutput of `join_items`, and whether that determination agrees with\n`joiner.py`.\n\n| behavior | probes exercising it | determined by joinspec.md? | agrees with joiner.py? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| separator | 1 (`[\"x\",\"y\"]`), 3, 5 | YES — \"Items are joined with ', ' (comma space).\" fixes the separator string exactly. | YES — joiner.py also uses `\", \"`. |\n| ordering | 1 (`[\"x\",\"y\"]`), 3 (`[\"one\",\"two\",\"three\"]`), 5 (`[\"b\",\"a\"]`) | YES — \"Items appear in the order given in the list.\" fixes as-given order. | NO — joiner.py applies `reversed(items)`, so on every probe with two or more items (indices 1, 3, 5) the spec-determined output differs from joiner.py's output. |\n| empty list | 2 (`[]`) | YES — \"An empty list renders as the empty string.\" | YES — `\", \".join(reversed([]))` is `\"\"`. |\n| single item | 0 (`[\"a\"]`), 4 (`[\"z\"]`) | YES — the separator is placed between consecutive items; a one-item list has no consecutive pair, so the output is the item itself. Ordering and the empty-list rule impose no further constraint. | YES — reversing a one-item list is the identity. |\n\n## Explicit argument that no behavior is undetermined\n\nEvery probe is a finite list of plain strings. The output of `join_items`\non such a list is a total function of (a) the separator placed between\nconsecutive items, (b) the order in which items appear, and (c) the\nrendering of the boundary case with no items. joinspec.md fixes all three:\nseparator `\", \"`, as-given order, empty list → `\"\"`. The single-item case\nis the degenerate instance of (a)+(b): with no consecutive pair there is\nno separator occurrence, so the output is the sole item. Therefore for\nevery probe input the spec fixes a unique output, and the set of\nundetermined behaviors on the probe set is **empty**.\n\n## E-A \"determines differently\" clause\n\nThe one behavior joinspec.md determines *differently from joiner.py* is\n**ordering**: the spec mandates as-given order while joiner.py emits items\nin reversed order. This affects exactly the probes with ≥ 2 items\n(indices 1, 3, 5). Whether the final verdict records these as divergent\nprobe indices is governed by E-B/E-C: it awaits the item-level comparison\nagainst the admissible clean-room implementation, which is assigned to the\nisolated child and integrated by the deterministic root step.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":580,"cache_read_input_tokens":41415,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":580},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1de03699-dc61-4048-ab5a-93988687e484","uuid":"4b796df8-015a-4e83-80c3-2a0650d08a69","timestamp":"2026-08-09T20:25:02.620Z","request_id":"req_011CdsoJ2UVwYCP9Nx2mbL8b"}
