o the output is the item itself. Ordering and the empty-list rule impose no further constraint. | YES — reversing a one-item list is the identity. |\n\n## Explicit argument that no behavior is undetermined\n\nEvery probe is a finite list of plain strings. The output of `join_items`\non such a list is a total function of (a) the separator placed between\nconsecutive items, (b) the order in which items appear, and (c) the\nrendering of the boundary case with no items. joinspec.md fixes all three:\nseparator `\", \"`, as-given order, empty list → `\"\"`. The single-item case\nis the degenerate instance of (a)+(b): with no consecutive pair there is\nno separator occurrence, so the output is the sole item. Therefore for\nevery probe input the spec fixes a unique output, and the set of\nundetermined behaviors on the probe set is **empty**.\n\n## E-A \"determines differently\" clause\n\nThe one behavior joinspec.md determines *differently from joiner.py* is\n**ordering**: the spec mandates as-given order while joiner.py emits items\nin reversed order. This affects exactly the probes with ≥ 2 items\n(indices 1, 3, 5). Whether the final verdict records these as divergent\nprobe indices is governed by E-B/E-C: it awaits the item-level comparison\nagainst the admissible clean-room implementation, which is assigned to the\nisolated child and integrated by the deterministic root step.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
